toons.strategies:
At first glance, Bassie seems to fit into the role of a distractor, having maximum Movement Speed and very high Stamina. However, because of her unique Abilities and 4 Stealth, she is best played as a survivalist/support Toon rather than a pure distractor. With that said, she may fit into the role of a backup distractor depending on your team's average Stealth and how experienced they are. She makes an extremely good survivalist due to her superior mobility stats, Stealth, and additional Item slot.

Due to Bassie's abysmal Extraction Speed and Skill Check stats, it is recommended to mostly avoid Machines and instead use her excellent survivability for your team's benefit. However, if there are any Treadmill Machines, Bassie is one of the best extractors for them, due to her extremely potent mobility Stats. She can also help with Duo Machines, though preferably only if you are good at hitting her tiny Skill Checks, as her below average Extraction Speed would be combined with another Toon's Extraction Speed, together totaling to a great minimum about the equivalent of 5 Stars or more.

Because of her Abilities that grant four inventory slots and enable her to drop Items without penalty, Bassie is an amazing team supporter and offers new ways for Toons to approach Floors. This is primarily done by storing Items for future Floors, or helping out fellow Toons by gifting them important Items such as Boxes o' Chocolates, Health Kits, or Smoke Bombs.

It's beneficial to give Toons Items that aid with their weaknesses, such as giving Rodger a Speed Candy, or collecting 'worthless' Items for a Sprout/ Ginger to get more Tapes. Furthermore, a strategy is to gather all Items on the Floor and leave them at certain locations, such as leaving Skill Check Candies and Extraction Speed Candies next to incomplete Machines, and leaving all other Items nearby the Elevator for others to grab during Panic Mode. Additionally, if Twisted Gigi is on the Floor, you can either drop your valuable Items in a safe spot or simply in front of the Elevator to avoid them being taken, though you either need Twisted Gigi occupied or your Items guarded due to her ability to take Items off the floor, rearrange them to let least valuable Items go first, or embrace it and act as delivery for any lost Items, maybe even transport Items between Toons more conveniently.

Stamina, Speed, and survival Items should be dropped in the loops/in front of struggling Toons/distractors, especially if they use an Item, freeing up space. They should also be provided to low-mobility Toons after they escape, as they may need more immediately afterwards. Leave any extras near the Elevator so Toons can reach them in time. You can also put these near Treadmill Machines so Toons can finish them faster.

As for heals, Bassie should be used to deliver Bandages and Health Kits to distractors so they don't have to leave their loops. She acts as storage for these, similarly to Cosmo, even if she can't "split" Health Kits. She should always buy heals when able. Generally, Bassie should prioritize Bandages and Cosmo should prioritize Health Kits if either of the Toons are present, but making them split these Items makes access to healing Items more efficient for your teammates, so try not to let one of them hog all of the healing Items. When in doubt, Bassie can always drop them for Cosmo later, can pick them up faster, and can hold onto more Items safely, so she can function as their second inventory.

Extraction Items can be delivered to Toons you notice working on Machines (especially if they free up space) or held onto if you intend to work on Machines yourself. Jumper Cables and Valves can be used yourself, as they offer the same value no matter who uses them. In fact, you can sprint to a Machine near a distractor and use them there so Toons don't need to go there/stay there for less time.

Due to her Ability synergizing with Trinkets related to picking up Items, Bassie has the potential to become very mobile, as she can constantly drop and pick up Items in order to activate either Bone's 25% Movement Speed bonus or Feather Duster's 20 Stamina unit regeneration. Bone's increase has the potential to make her one of the fastest Toons in the game, outpacing even the likes of Looey at 1 or Eclipse in a Blackout with really good timing, as picking up too often (yet also not often enough) can drop your speed boost down to 6.7%. Feather Duster can basically give Bassie infinite Stamina, but this is of course once again dependent on timing. Overall, doing this can require a lot of focus, so try to practice before using these techniques in a proper run.

Bassie is incredible at support and survival, with a strong emphasis on team gameplay. She is a great choice if you can stay on top of supporting and communicating with teammates. Being able to adapt practically any Item into being useful by giving it to the right Toon.

**Trinket Combinations**

Recommended Trinket combinations for Bassie:

**Mobility**

• **Bone and Feather Duster/Any**

•With these Trinkets, by dropping and picking up Items quickly, Bassie gains extraordinary amounts of speed. This combination is often used to distract as Bassie, though it's only recommended in specific teams that have Toons with higher Stealth.

• **Dog Plush/Options**

•This Trinket alone allows Bassie to outwalk anything besides Twisted Pebble, Twisted Glisten when enraged, Twisted Coal when a Blackout is present, and some others (22 walk speed). It's also an alternative to Bone and Feather Duster if one does not have the ability to constantly pick up Items to gain speed easily. Here are some options to pair Dog Plush with:

• **Speedy Shoes**, to further improve walk speed, more preferable for those new to distracting as Bassie (23.1 walk speed);

• **Feather Duster**, to further improve stamina management if you do not have Bone, more preferable when against fast Twisteds like Twisted Pebble for those new to distracting as Bassie;

• **Clown Horn/ Ribbon Spool**, to also out-walk before Panic Mode Twisted Glisten, Twisted Pebble, and Twisted Vee when you are inflicted with the Slow II Debuff when active (24.2 walk/33 run speed). Also good if you plan on duo-distracting;

• **Train Whistle**, to completely nullify the Slow Debuff, making Twisted Vee significantly easier to distract;

• **Vanity Mirror**, to more easily and safely gather any secret stashed items during Panic Mode (39 run speed);

•This is only recommended if you have unorganized teammates and must stash your items far away from the Elevator;

• **Scrapbook**, to avoid other 4 Stealth Toons taking away the Twisteds' aggro;

•Do note that this Trinket will not lower your Stealth enough to be able to distract around 3 Star Stealth and below Toons.

•Any of the Trinkets in the **Extraction Section**, to help with Floor progression; etc.

**Extraction**

•The following Trinkets help Bassie deal with her main deficiency, that being her 1 Skill Check and 2 Star Extraction Speed:

• **Vee's Remote**, to instantly complete Machines in dangerous locations, such as dead ends and open areas;

• **Mime Makeup**, to more safely extract by removing the danger of missing Skill Checks;

• **Thinking Cap**, to increase Skill Check Size, helping to fail less Skill Checks, though Mime Makeup can be more beneficial in some cases;

•If the player has no issues with small Skill Checks, they may consider using **Magnifying Glass** or **Participation Award** to help with Machine extraction more easily, even if the Skill Check window is far smaller. Though, do note that if the player has lag/missing frames, they may want to consider other Trinkets for team progression, as Bassie is typically better with Treadmill Machines.

•Any Trinkets listed in the **Mobility** section, to help with survival.

**Support**

• **Dandy Plush**, to allow Bassie to stock up on important Items earlier and continuously for half the cost so they can be shared with her teammates;

• **Memory Locket**, to help Bassie find and store healing Items for her team.

**Note: Do not use Sweet Charm or Ghost Snakes In A Can on Bassie, as they do not lower her cooldown. Additionally, Peppermint Icing does not work with her Ability.**

Cosmo is a supporter whose Active Ability, **Sharing is Caring**, gives one of his to another damaged Toon. As one of only four Toons capable of healing at the moment, his Ability is nice in situations where a teammate is low on Hearts or at risk. Be cautious though, giving out too many Hearts will quickly put you in danger and might even cause harm to teammates in an attempt to save you.

With a good set of Stats, the main issue for Cosmo is his 1 Skill Check. The small window makes them much more difficult, and the value given makes them not very rewarding when landing them. Even though Cosmo is the fastest at extraction compared to Bassie, Blot, and Shrimpo, all four of them still have 1 Star Skill Check, making it arduous to even get a good result on the Machine. This disadvantage can be managed by using Skill Check Trinkets like Mime Makeup, Thinking Cap, or Blue Bandana (though note Blue Bandana slows progress down by a bit and is only recommended to newer players). On the other hand, Cosmo's above-average Stealth and mobility makes it much easier to go and heal Distractors, as outside of turning corners, distracted Twisteds will not switch targets, and even so, he can easily escape them. If you fail Skill Checks often, extracting from Treadmill Machines can be quite good, especially thanks to his higher Stamina. Due to Cosmo not filling any other niche (other than support), it is recommended for you to just wander around the map for Tapes (assuming no Tape-reliant Toons are present and need them) and healing Items for later use. If the map has been scavenged already, check for teammates low on Health or find Machines to do.

He's well-suited to runs with a lot of Main Character Toons present due to his Ability to stretch Health Kit twice as far for them. His long range normally can make it a bit difficult to heal the right teammate, though if in the aforementioned team setup, any heal will go towards a Toon on 1 Heart.

Cosmo's best benefit over the other healers is his Ability's secondary effect: highlighting selected teammates through walls and showing which animation they're in. Since his range is extremely long, you can keep tabs on others from a great distance. This is helpful to detect other players, such as distractors or players in need of assistance.

When looking for Items, focus on Health Kits and Bandages so you can make use of your Ability more often. Since Cosmo can redistribute Hearts on the fly, consider asking your teammates to alert you if they see any so you can bring the Hearts to distractors or save them for later Floors. Also allow those in need of healing to take Items since Cosmo's higher Stamina and Stealth leave him in less danger. Since Health Kits and Bandages are quite rare, you can also look for more common but helpful Items such as Stopwatches to make his Skill Check more bearable. The extra Tapes received from them can also be used to buy healing Items when they are being sold. If a non-Main Character teammate is on 1 Heart, it is recommended to let them take a Health Kit if they happen to find it on the floor, as it instantly heals them as compared to your heal of 1 Heart.

If Bassie is in your group, try to prioritize Health Kits and give them any Bandages. The more people capable of delivering healing, the faster ailing Toons receive aid. You can also rely on Bassie to hold onto most healing Items and to heal you once your Health is low, as she out-speeds you. Leaving your heals with a Bassie can also be very beneficial in the event that Twisted Gigi spawns on the floor as Bassie can repeatedly deposit and pick up the Items, rendering Twisted Gigi unable to take them with good timing.

Cosmo is great when paired with Ginger, as Ginger can heal him while at low Health, essentially making him an infinite healing machine. This, however, would require a lot of Tape income for Ginger, depending on how much your team loses Hearts, so it isn't as optimal as it seems. He can also pair up with Sprout but less effectively, as he can only heal with 1 Heart with his Ability for the same cost.

Overall, Cosmo's Stats make him most preferably played as a healer who utilizes his longest range out of the three to heal from a safe place, but still also decently contribute with roughly any Machine types (primarily if you don't struggle with his 1 Star Skill Check). He acts somewhat like an all-rounder in comparison to the other healers.

**Trinket Combinations**

Some Trinkets sets for Cosmo includes:

**Survivability**

• **Pink Bow/Options**

•This Trinket enables Cosmo to outrun all Twisteds before Panic Mode besides Twisted Dyle as well as reaching teammates at max speed faster (26.9 run speed). Here are some options to pair Pink Bow with;

• **Speedy Shoes**, to further improve Run Speed (28.2 run speed);

• **Vanity Mirror**, to outrun every Twisted except Twisted Dyle during Panic Mode (35 run speed);

• **Diary**, although quite situational, can help if there is a distractor with 4 Stealth such as Bassie so you can safely go near them to collect heals while they distract;

• **Savory Charm**, to be a tad bit safer if on 1, due to Cosmo's frequent Heart loss, despite the usage of this Trinket only working once;

• **Train Whistle**, to help Cosmo avoid losing Hearts during an Ichor Leak, or while Twisted Vee on the Floor;

•Any of the Trinkets in the **Extraction Section**; etc.

**Extraction**

•The following Trinkets help Cosmo extract by dealing with his main deficiency, which is his 1 Skill Check (50 skill check size) and 3 Star Extraction Speed (1.00 extraction speed):

• **Vee's Remote**, to instantly complete Machines in dangerous locations, such as dead ends and open areas;

• **Mime Makeup**, to more safely extract by removing the danger of missing Skill Checks;

• **Thinking Cap**, to increase Skill Check Size, helping to fail less Skill Checks, though Mime Makeup can be more beneficial in some cases;

•If the player has no issues with small Skill Checks, they may consider using **Magnifying Glass** or **Participation Award** to help with Machine extraction more easily, even if the Skill Check window is far smaller. Though, do note that if the player has lag/missing frames, they may want to consider other Trinkets for team progression.

•Any Trinkets listed in the **Survivability/Mobility** section, to help with survival.

**Ability/Support**

• **Ghost Snakes In A Can**, to reduce **Sharing is Caring'**s Ability cooldown (55s);

• **Sweet Charm**, to further reduce **Sharing is Caring**'s cooldown, though only by a small amount as the Trinket works with percentages (50.2s).

• **Memory Locket**, to help highlight any healing Items on the Floor, making it easier to heal other teammates;

•This Trinket is best used in unorganized runs where heals may be easily taken by other players.

• **Dandy Plush**, to help you buy healing Items if a Toon that needs Tapes (e.g. Ginger) is present, letting you still have sufficient Tapes from usual activities for healing Items.

**Note: It isn't recommended to use Tape-giving Trinkets such as Fancy Purse, as it is a one-time use Trinket, and wastes a Trinket slot if Dandy does not sell a Health Kit or Bandage on an earlier floor before you have 100 Tapes. Trinkets such as Dandy Plush are more beneficial in the long run, as their effects last the whole run.**

Yatta is an extremely good hybrid Toon, due to her high Movement Speed and Extraction Speed, along with her low Stealth. Her Passive Ability, **Piñata Party**, is useful for both self-sustain and support. What Candy Items she can drop is based on a probability system; Yatta can drop all Candy Items, excluding BonBons. Candies she can drop are Chocolate, Gumballs, Stamina Candy, Stealth Candy, Extraction Speed Candy, Skill Check Candy, Speed Candy, Jawbreaker, and Box o' Chocolates, with a Box o' Chocolates having a 5.88% chance to drop while all other Candy Items each have an 11.76% chance to drop. 2 or 4 Items will drop depending on if she finishes a Machine (2) or gets hit (4), which roughly would mean around 1 Box o' Chocolates every 10 Machines, or around a 20% chance upon getting hit. If you are working on a Machine and see Yatta nearby, consider priming it and letting her finish it to activate her Passive more easily, giving your team access to extra Items.

Due to her horrid Stamina capacity, saving Items such as Chocolates is rather decent as it can increase her Stamina and Walk Speed for a short time, and other Stamina-related Items, such as Pop, or Bottle o' Pop, are also helpful to accommodate her Stamina. Voting for Well-Paced/Endurance can also help with her low Stamina, raising her total to 120, close to the 2 Star Stamina level of 125. Completing Dyle's Floor is a priority for Yatta players.

Yatta's Ability works nicely with the Toons mentioned below:

•Having a Yatta in your team with a Sprout or Ginger can help with the Tape earning, as she can drop worthless candy for Sprout/Ginger to pick up for extra Tapes, or candy that has use and which grants more Tapes, like Box o' Chocolates or Jawbreakers, helping them heal Toons faster. Yatta's Items are also a good Tape source for Teagan and Blot, although they don't need to prioritize Tape gathering as much.

•Pebble and Coal's Item-finding Abilities can help detect the Items Yatta produces much easier, if they land above ground and/or are lodged inside of an object.

• Bassie can carry the Items Yatta produces, preventing them from taking up other Toons' inventory slots. This also allows Bassie to distribute them strategically (e.g. Skill Check Candies to those working on Machines, Box o' Chocolates to help distractors or Toons that are slow/have low stamina) to avoid waste.

Because Yatta's Passive frequently generates Items, it's easy for your inventory to fill up quickly. To avoid this, try to dedicate at least one inventory slot to her candy drops. If your inventory is full, consider leaving Items like Speed Candies or Boxes o' Chocolates for slower teammates who can benefit instead.

Yatta can also be good for distracting with her 4 Movement Speed and 2 Star Stealth. Although Stamina management is hard, equipping the Dog Plush/ Speedy Shoes combo lets you outwalk a little over half of the Twisteds in-game and match 5 Star Movement Speed. However, if it's a serious issue, either get Stamina Items from her Ability or use a Stamina Trinket. She's better suited as a backup distractor in most cases anyway, considering she'd be able to utilize her 5 Star Extraction Speed and Ability more frequently, and the fact other Toons like Goob (whose stats are a direct increase for purely distracting) may be more viable for the role.

Yatta is great for solos, with her excellent extraction Stats and mobility. Despite her high speed, her low Stamina can be dangerous if getting chased for a long period of time, especially with fast Twisteds such as Twisted Shelly and Twisted Toodles. Using Pink Bow will give her enough Run Speed to get away from Twisteds before losing most of her Stamina, though the capacity makes it used seldomly.

Yatta's Passive can help a lot by providing a consistent source of Tapes from candy, as well as the candy itself providing benefits. However, your inventory could be filled up quite quickly, and even if it's with good drops like Box o' Chocolates, always save space for other useful Items like Bandages/Health Kits, escaping Items, and/or Valves.

If playing with a 2 Star Stealth distractor, Yatta can afford to fill 1 or 2 slots with Stealth Candies, as she's easily able to recover heaps of decent Items to refill the slots with and will continue to generate Stealth Candies over time.

Yatta's Colorful April Skin is slightly advantageous in a Spring Fever, as its tails can be used to gauge which effect Gumballs or a Jawbreaker have given the player under the color-altered conditions.

**Trinket Combinations**

Recommended Trinket combinations for Yatta:

**Mobility**

• **Dog Plush/Options**

•This Trinket will unlock the role of distractor to the holder. (19.3 walk speed) Here are some options to pair Dog Plush with:

• **Speedy Shoes**, to allow Yatta to outwalk Twisteds slower than Twisted Toodles and Twisted Shelly (20.2 walk speed);

• **Clown Horn**/ **Ribbon Spool**, to out-walk even faster Twisteds. Keep in mind this only works half the time, and is best used when duo-distracting (21 walk speed);

•Do note this Trinket combo is best used when duo-distracting with another player.

• **Vanity Mirror**, to out-run almost all Twisteds during Panic Mode while active (35.75 run speed);

• **Train Whistle**, to completely nullify the Slow Debuff, making Twisted Vee significantly easier to distract, and Ichor Leaks much easier to distract in;

• **Friendship Bracelet**, to be able to sprint for longer periods without a speed debuff, though reliant on multiple alive players. Can take advantage of Yatta's Candy Item spawning, maintaining survivability of the team;

• **Diary**, to support 2 distractors without taking off Twisteds' aggro;

•Only use this Trinket combination if your distractor has 2 Star Stealth.

• **Scrapbook**, to be able to distract without other 2 Stealth Toons taking aggro off you;

•Only use this Trinket combo if most of the players in your run have 2 Star Stealth, or if you're solar distracting with a Bobette lacking Diary.

• **Star Pillow**, to regenerate Stamina faster while extracting, taking advantage of her low Stamina and fast extraction;

•Any of the Trinkets in the **Extraction Section**, to help with Floor progression; etc.

• **Bone/Options**

•This Trinket allows Yatta to take advantage of the candy she drops. Here are some other options to pair Bone with:

• **Glazed Fondant Bag**, to make Yatta's candy duration time longer, as she can take advantage of it. She can also gain twice the speed if picking up a speed candy and using it at the same time;

• **Feather Duster**, to easily regain Stamina, can be handy when in the need to escape a Twisted after being hit and low on Stamina;

• **Whispering Flower**, to regenerate Stamina with the number of Items in your inventory, this can be handy along with her Passive Ability.

**Extraction**

• **Magnifying Glass/Options**

•This Trinket allows Yatta to take full advantage over her 5 Extraction Speed (1.5 extraction speed) and 3 Star Skill Check (100.5 skill check size). Here are some options to pair Magnifying Glass with:

• **Vee's Remote**, to allow for quicker Floor progression, especially if teammates also carry Vee's Remote;

• **Participation Award**, to speed up Machine completion by maximizing Skill Check extraction (19.91 avg/17.7 with Tech Savvy);

• **Stress Ball**, to increase Extraction Speed gradually every successful Skill Check hit, supporting her 5 Star Extraction Speed and stacking with associated candy boosts;

•Any Trinkets listed in the **Mobility** section, to help with survival.

Connie could be considered the best survivalist Toon in the game, having the highest Stealth in the game alongside an Ability that's quite helpful against escaping dangerous Twisteds. She also has above average Extraction Speed, making her great for those who want to extract more efficiently. However, Connie possesses low mobility Statistics, that being 1 Movement Speed and an average Stamina capacity, along with below average Skill Check, needing some skill to play her safely.

Connie's lowest Stat, being Movement Speed, is something that should be mitigated in a way or another, as by herself, she cannot outrun most Twisteds in the game. For that reason alone, keeping Movement Speed Items like Boxes o' Chocolates or Speed Candies in your inventory, using Trinkets such as Pink Bow, and getting speed boosts from support Toons is a few of the the things you could do in order to make her better to move around more easily.

Focusing on her main Ability, **Haunting Escape**, one can see it's potential in helping the player out of sticky situations, such as being chased by a Twisted that Connie can't out-run (eg. Twisted Shelly or Twisted Toodles). Her Ability is, in a way, an infinite source of Smoke Bombs, having a somewhat short cooldown with a short duration. Though great on the surface, some issues may arise from it, as it does not work on Ranged or Lethal Twisteds, so instead of using her Ability on them, hiding or avoiding being seen by any of those in the aforementioned categories can save you a life.

However, Connie is more than her Ability. In addition to having the highest Stealth in the game, she has immunity to Ichor Leaks and produces light during Blackouts, making her even better for wandering around as well as surviving during Floor Events. On top of those, her good extractability makes her great for extracting Machines that are near distractors and other Machines that are in dangerous spots, so long as you don't miss a Skill Check or more, given that the Stat is subpar. Her Ichor Puddle Immunity in general is also useful when it comes to Twisted Finn and Twisted Tisha (situationally), as their special abilities can make traversal or extraction more difficult due to the Debuffs. Illness would normally be an issue, especially with Connie's 2 Star Skill Check, but she can't be affected by the -50% size decrease, and thus no worry of an involuntary, significant size decrease.

Despite all of the upsides mentioned above, Connie is less than adequate for completing Dyle's Floor, because, as aforementioned, her Ability doesn't work on Twisted Dyle, and nor does she have a chance at escaping him if in his line of sight for too long. While completing his Floor as her is not impossible, it's definitely a hard task, and there are better options out there if you wish to complete his Floor with ease. Even if you somehow escape, her increased Stamina can't be utilized to the max, as although she can sprint more, she can't sprint faster, so running away from faster Twisteds is still harder.

Overall, Connie is a great survivalist and decent extractor, yet struggles with mobility, which can be somewhat alleviated in one way or another.

**Trinket Combinations**

For specific Trinket recommendations, here are some possible combinations:

**Survivability**

• **Pink Bow/Options**

•This Trinket enables Connie to outrun some Twisteds before Panic Mode (21.5 run speed). Here are some options to pair Pink Bow with;

• **Speedy Shoes**, to further increase Run Speed (22.6 run speed), making her be able to outrun all average speed Twisteds even on Panic Mode, albeit by a tiny amount;

• **Vanity Mirror**, to be able to outrun every Twisted except Twisted Pebble during Panic Mode (27.9 run speed);

• **Train Whistle**, to completely nullify the Slow Debuff, making Twisted Vee significantly easier to survive;

• **Peppermint Icing**, to give her a bit of extra Stamina to get to a safe spot away from Twisteds while invisible;

• **Star Pillow**, to regenerate Stamina faster while extracting, taking advantage of her average Stamina and fast extraction;

•The Trinkets listed in the **Extraction** section, to help with Floor progression; etc.

**Extraction**

• **Magnifying Glass/Options**

•This Trinket allows Connie to take full advantage over her 4 Extraction Speed (1.2 extraction speed) and 2 Star Skill Check (67 skill check size). Here are some options to pair Magnifying Glass with:

• **Vee's Remote**, to allow for quicker Floor progression, especially if teammates also carry Vee's Remote;

• **Participation Award**, to speed up Machine completion by maximizing Skill Check extraction. However, it leaves you with a near 1 Window Size (24.19 avg/21.51 with Tech Savvy);

• **Thinking Cap**, to increase Skill Check size, helping you hit your Skill Checks easier if you struggle with such;

• **Mime Makeup**, to avoid attracting Twisteds to your location when you miss a Skill Check, also helps if you desire to complete Dyle's Floor at ease;

•Any Trinkets listed in the **Survivability/Mobility** section, to help with survival.

**Ability**

• **Ghost Snakes In A Can**, to reduce **Haunting Escape'**s Ability cooldown (45s).

• **Sweet Charm**, to further reduce **Haunting Escape**'s cooldown, though only by a medium amount as the Trinket works with percentages (41s).

Soulvester has the best extraction in game, with 5 in both Skill Check and Extraction Speed, along with a very strong Ability that can save teammates. However, this comes at the cost of being one of the worst Toons in terms of mobility, having 1 Star Movement Speed and Stamina, matching mobility Stats with Shrimpo. Therefore, playing recklessly is much more risky than it usually would be, so caution and patience will be key to playing Soulvester right.

Soulvester may be best used in a team with a distractor present. This will allow him to fully utilize his high extraction stats and keep him away from danger, though his life could be in jeopardy if the distractor dies. In general, caution should be taken with faster Twisteds like Twisted Shelly and Twisted Toodles as it can be hard to escape them. A good strategy is to always check corners and behind walls, and position your camera in a position where you can easily spot Twisteds while doing Machines.

Where Soulvester can really shine outside of his incredible extraction is his Ability, **Soul Shield**. Soulvester sacrifices a to protect a teammate (or himself in a solo run) from one Twisted attack, preserving their Hearts (or the rest of his own Hearts). When his Ability is used, the targeted Toon is given Guarded (and constantly highlighted to Soulvester), while Soulvester himself gets GuardStance I (speed increased to 12.5 walking/22.5 running); both of these Buffs will be removed when the Toon with Guarded is attacked by a Twisted. Take note that the indicator icon above your Ability button will tell you whether the Guarded Toon is in a chase or not, the background will turn red in a chase; valiant music will also play during the Guarded Toon's chase, fading out once the chase has ended. After Soulvester's Ability successfully defends from an attack and he teleports to the defended Toon, he gains GuardBurst (speed increased to 17.5 walking/27.5 running) for 2 seconds and Untargetable for 3 seconds; use this time to get away from Twisteds or get into the Elevator if it's Panic Mode/almost Panic Mode. When hit by a Twisted normally, Soulvester gains HurtBurst (increasing speed to 15 walking/25 running) for 2 seconds.

Twisteds are stunned for an extra 1.5 seconds (stacked with their attack cooldown) after attacking a defended Toon, so it could be a viable strategy to use the Guarded Buff and get attacked on purpose to stun particularly dangerous Twisteds like Twisted Pebble. In general though, be careful about giving your Hearts out too quickly (unless there is a healer Toon who can heal you, though this could potentially be a waste), as not only could you end up giving your Hearts to players who die quickly despite the protection; you can put yourself on 1 Heart too quickly (not only leaving you in danger, but also preventing you from defending your teammates since the Ability can't be used on 1 Heart). If you receive a protection from a Soulvester teammate, be careful about how you use it, since you could end up wasting it and endangering him when he teleports to you. Goob can be a good teammate to have on a team, as he can pull Soulvester away from a Twisted after stunning them. Please note that Lethal Twisteds will still kill a Guarded Toon. If Soulvester is far from the Elevator, without the means to make it back during Panic Mode, it's a viable strategy for the Guarded Toon to get attacked by a Twisted near the Elevator on purpose, as Soulvester will get teleported to safety.

When guarding someone, keep in mind that you can only guard one person at a time, and each Toon can only have one Soulvester guarding them (the Ability will not work if another Soulvester is already guarding your target). If you change who you are defending, or the person you're currently defending leaves the game, you will not be refunded any spent health, so you have to be very careful to not waste it.

Guarded Toons are immune to being hurt by Twisted Goob and Twisted Scraps's ranged attacks, making them much less of a threat, especially Twisted Scraps, as she doesn't have Goob's longer attention span (and ability to displace) which should still be noted. Taking this into consideration, Guarded Toons can safely bait ranged attacks from Twisted Scraps/Goob to put the Twisted(s) on cooldown if they're near the Elevator; the Guarded Toon can also force the ranged attack cooldown if they and/or their teammates are about to be cornered, allowing for an escape, especially with the stun.

Using the Ability on distractors (especially Looey on 1 Heart), Main Character Toons, and healers can be a good choice as they are invaluable to the team or vulnerable. Healers can heal you up after you protect them or another Toon too, but be careful since you could eat through healers' Abilities (which all require either Hearts or Tapes, limited resources). Additionally, with Looey, he can get to 1 Heart and get protected to basically have 2 Hearts, making distracting really easy. Do remember that since Soulvester will teleport, he could take aggro away from distractors after the Untargetable runs out, so equipping Diary can be a good idea if you intend on defending any 3 Star Stealth distractors. Also note that this teleport can pull him away from a Machine he's extracting, which could be a bit jarring. The Twisted stun could potentially also screw up a distractor, but it may be worth it if it means saving their life. Getting into the Elevator as soon as possible during Panic Mode will prevent you from being teleported far away if the Guarded Toon gets attacked. If you block damage from within the Elevator, your Ability will be on cooldown until the next Floor.

Like his sister, Soulvester has more advantages in Floor Events compared to other Toons, being Light-Producing in Blackouts and possessing immunity to the Illness III, Slow I, and Ichor Stains applied by Ichor Leaks (as well as immunity to Twisted Finn's Ichor Puddles). Overall, he's a nice option if Floor Events are difficult.

If you regularly play Connie and adapt to her similarly poor Movement Speed, transitioning to Soulvester could be a good idea, because you'd not only know how to deal with his 1 Star Movement Speed, but also receive the bonuses mentioned before (max extraction, stunning Ability, speed increasing Ability, etc.). Remember this comes at the cost of less survivability compared to Connie (2 less Stars in both Stamina and Stealth compared to her, plus no invisibility Ability), as well as a more support oriented play-style so be aware.

Due to his low mobility, you may consider spawning Twisted Dyle. However, it would be quite difficult. His awful mobility will not just be hard to manage against Twisted Dyle, but also the Common Twisteds that spawn alongside him. However, his max extraction can make the Floor go faster, so he could be an asset, though wandering too far from the Elevator would be risky, so it is advised to have the faster/stealthier Toons disperse while you finish up the closer Machines. When completing Dyle's Floor, you will finally gain the needed Stamina. The extra Stamina can allow him to use his speed boost from his Ability more often too.

If intending to play in a solo run as Soulvester, making use of his Ability will take care of his mobility. It's best to save the Ability for when you either are getting low on Stamina, are being chased by an extremely fast Twisted, or are being chased by a Twisted with a really high attention span. By saving the Ability for chases, you'll keep your Health up for longer while being able to stun Twisteds who actually need to be stunned. You could also choose to just use the Ability immediately, giving yourself the equivalent of 2 Star Movement Speed near constantly, but note that this can mean putting yourself on 2 Hearts (effectively becoming a Main Character Stat-wise) or even 1 Heart. Even though in solo runs Soulvester doesn't do a cutscene for defending from an attack, he still will get the Untargetable Buff for 3 seconds, which can let you escape more easily when combined with the stun. Either way, a lot of patience is required when soloing as Soulvester, as the mobility can drag things out, or make it difficult to avoid Twisteds; for this reason, it's recommend to really only solo as him if you have the patience or even actively enjoy playing as lower speed Toons.

The best Items to focus on are those that can help with Movement Speed and Stamina, in order to remedy Soulvester's minimal mobility. Anything helps, even the extra 25 Stamina capacity from a Chocolate could mean the difference between life and death. Try to stay consistently stocked up on at least one or two mobility based Items, as his extraction is already extremely potent. Using extraction related Items like Instructions can be pretty great too, as they'll multiply with his high extraction Stats, resulting in finishing Machines very quickly, just make sure you still prioritize mobility. Staying stocked up on at least one Healing Item is a good idea, so you can support teammates more often (or use the Ability on yourself without as much risk). A Bandage may be less useful than a Health Kit, since the latter can heal 2 (3 with Soul Sword) Hearts immediately while the Bandage provides 1 Heart only, though if you take a Bandage you can use it immediately without wasting it, opening up space for mobility Items; in the end it depends on whether you want an immediate heal after using your Ability, or to have a larger sum of Hearts in storage for multiple Ability usages. Healing Items in general are also just useful due to Soulvester's minimal mobility, with either of the two Items being good to keep on hand, though it's preferable to take Health Kits if there are Main Character teammates, since they would otherwise waste the Hearts given.

Overall, Soulvester is a decent Toon to go as, though ensuring you reach higher Floors requires a bit of assistance from other teammates. He is a great supporter who can save those in need, but this does mean sacrificing his own Hearts. He has a few resistances, immunities, and advantages, only really being held back by his mobility. Careful gameplay, awareness, being smart with how you sacrifice your Hearts, and making every piece of Stamina count is the optimal way to ensure Soulvester is able to utilize his strengths.

